\description{
\code{bartisan()} accepts the \link[stats:family]{stats::family} objects used by \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}}, so
\code{gaussian()}, \code{binomial("probit")}, \code{poisson()} and \code{\link[stats:Gamma]{stats::Gamma()}} all work
unchanged. The functions documented here supply the additional families that
have no \code{glm()} counterpart, in the same style, so that they can be passed to
the \code{family} argument the same way.

One thing to know about the gamma family, because base R's default is the
wrong choice here: \code{stats::Gamma()} defaults to \code{link = "inverse"}, and the
inverse link is the worst link for this sampler. \strong{Write \code{Gamma("log")}.}
Base R's function is left as base R defines it, so that attaching this package
cannot change what \code{glm()} does; naming the family as a string,
\code{family = "Gamma"}, gets the log link, since that spelling is this package's
own. \code{bartisan()} says so when a link's
inverse does not cover the additive predictor, which is the case that catches
it; see Details.
